---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import Histogram from "../../components/Histogram.astro";
import PriceChart from "../../components/PriceChart.astro";

const title = "RSI Explained — Relative Strength Index";
const description =
  "How RSI(14) is calculated, what overbought and oversold actually mean, why " +
  "RSI stays extreme in a strong trend, and how to read it on the screener.";
---

<Base title={title} description={description} crumb="Technical analysis / RSI">
  <h1>RSI — Relative Strength Index</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    RSI compares the size of recent gains with the size of recent losses and
    expresses the result on a 0–100 scale. It measures the <em>balance</em> of
    buying and selling pressure over a lookback window — nothing more.
  </p>

  <PriceChart
    ticker="RELIANCE" indicator="rsi" levels={[30, 50, 70]}
    shadeAbove={70} shadeBelow={30}
    markPeak markTrough peakNote="highest RSI" troughNote="lowest RSI"
    title="What RSI looks like underneath a real price chart"
    caption="Price on top, RSI(14) below. The dotted lines mark the highest and lowest RSI readings of the period — follow each one up to the price line and see what price was doing at that moment. This is the whole idea: RSI is a description of the move above it, not a separate prediction." />

  <h2>How it is calculated</h2>
  <div class="formula">RS  = Average gain over n periods / Average loss over n periods
RSI = 100 − 100 / (1 + RS)</div>
  <p>
    The default <span class="c">n</span> is 14. Averages are smoothed (Wilder's
    method) rather than simple, so each new bar has a modest effect and the line
    does not jump about.
  </p>
  <p class="lede">
    The formula has one consequence worth internalising: RSI can only reach 100 if
    there have been <em>no</em> down periods in the window, and 0 if there have
    been no up periods. Both extremes are effectively unreachable in real markets,
    which is why 70 and 30 became the conventional thresholds rather than 90 and 10.
  </p>

  <h2>What "overbought" does not mean</h2>
  <p>
    RSI above 70 is conventionally called overbought and below 30 oversold. These
    are descriptions of recent price behaviour, not instructions, and the most
    common mistake in all of technical analysis is treating them as signals to
    sell and buy.
  </p>
  <p class="lede">
    <strong>In a strong trend, RSI stays extreme for a long time.</strong> A stock
    in a powerful advance can hold above 70 for weeks while continuing to rise.
    Selling because "RSI is overbought" means selling precisely the strongest
    thing you own, and buying at 30 in a genuine downtrend means buying repeatedly
    into a decline that keeps going.
  </p>
  <p>
    That is why the <a href="/screener/">screener</a>'s default condition looks for
    RSI <em>between</em> 35 and 70 rather than below 30 — it excludes both
    extremes rather than chasing either.
  </p>

  <Histogram
    field="rsi" min={0} max={100} bins={25}
    bands={[{ from: 0, to: 30, label: "oversold", tone: "bad" },
            { from: 70, to: 100, label: "overbought", tone: "good" }]}
    markers={[30, 70]}
    title="Where RSI actually sits across the NSE universe"
    caption="Most stocks cluster in the middle on any given day. The tails are thin, which is precisely why 30 and 70 became the conventional lines rather than 10 and 90. On a strongly trending day the whole distribution shifts right." />

  <h2>Period length changes everything</h2>
  <div class="defs">
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>shorter_period</code><span class="meta">RSI(7) or less</span></div>
      <p>More reactive, hits extremes constantly, far more false readings. Each
        new bar carries more weight.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>standard</code><span class="meta">RSI(14)</span></div>
      <p>Wilder's original. Its dominance is convention rather than any proof of
        optimality — it is what most software defaults to, so it is what most
        people are reacting to.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>longer_period</code><span class="meta">RSI(21) or more</span></div>
      <p>Smoother, reaches extremes rarely, later. Fewer false readings and fewer
        readings overall.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Divergence, and the honest caveat</h2>
  <p>
    Divergence is when price makes a higher high but RSI makes a lower high, or
    the reverse at a low. The interpretation is that momentum is weakening even as
    price extends.
  </p>
  <p class="lede">
    It is widely taught and worth knowing about. It is also <strong>far easier to
    identify after the fact than in advance</strong>: divergences appear
    repeatedly during trends that simply continue, and only the ones that preceded
    a reversal get shown in the examples. Treat it as an observation, not a signal.
  </p>

  <h2>On the screener</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    The <span class="c">rsi</span> column is RSI(14) on daily closes, computed
    after the session. The default band of 35–70 is a starting point you can
    change with the slider — and changing it is instructive. Narrow it to 50–60
    and watch how few stocks survive. That collapse is the point: a screen looks
    selective mostly because of where the thresholds were drawn, not because it
    found something.
  </p>

  <div class="ad">Advertisement</div>

  <h2>What RSI cannot tell you</h2>
  <div class="defs">
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>direction_of_the_trend</code><span class="meta">use a moving average</span></div>
      <p>RSI is a momentum oscillator, not a trend indicator. An RSI of 55 says
        nothing about whether price is above or below its 200-day average. See
        <a href="/learn/moving-averages/">moving averages</a>.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>whether_a_trend_exists</code><span class="meta">use ADX</span></div>
      <p>RSI behaves very differently in a trending market than a sideways one,
        and it will not tell you which you are in.
        <a href="/learn/adx/">ADX</a> is the tool for that.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>anything_about_the_business</code><span class="meta">separate question</span></div>
      <p>RSI is a function of past prices. It has no knowledge of earnings, debt
        or management. See <a href="/learn/reading-financials/">reading a
        financial statement</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="lede">
    Next: <a href="/learn/adx/">ADX</a> ·
    <a href="/learn/moving-averages/">moving averages</a> ·
    <a href="/learn/volume/">volume and relative volume</a>
  </p>
</Base>
